<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mikael Sehul</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1691–1779</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mikael Sehul was a Tigrayan lord who dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a> in the late 18th century. Having built a strong regional state in Tigray that controlled the trade route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, had Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iyoas-i">Iyoas I</a> killed in 1769 and installed and removed emperors at will. His seizure of power is usually taken as the beginning of the Era of Princes. Driven out of Gondar in 1771, he spent his last years in Tigray.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mikael-sehul.jpg" alt="Mikael Sehul" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Bruce · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ras_Mikael_Sehul_burst_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
